1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:06,312
Nearly four thousand metres up in the Bolivian Andes, a stone lies in the grass.

2
00:00:06,312 --> 00:00:07,134
It is an H.

3
00:00:07,134 --> 00:00:13,338
Its corners are so sharp, and its faces so flat, that people ask a simple question.

4
00:00:13,338 --> 00:00:14,833
Could hands do this?

5
00:00:15,300 --> 00:00:17,165
Some say lost technology.

6
00:00:17,165 --> 00:00:19,253
Some say ancient astronauts.

7
00:00:19,253 --> 00:00:24,252
Others say patient stoneworkers with sand, stone hammers, and time.

8
00:00:24,252 --> 00:00:29,399
Tonight we audit the stones, clause by clause, and see what survives.

9
00:00:29,866 --> 00:00:31,342
The rules are simple.

10
00:00:31,342 --> 00:00:32,466
Evidence counts.

11
00:00:32,466 --> 00:00:34,855
Theories are labelled as theories.

12
00:00:34,855 --> 00:00:39,633
And at the end, every claim gets a stamp: pass, fail, or still open.

13
00:00:40,100 --> 00:00:41,958
This is Ancient Machines.

14
00:00:41,958 --> 00:00:44,113
The Puma Punku masonry audit.

15
00:00:44,113 --> 00:00:46,566
Clause one: where exactly are we?

16
00:00:47,033 --> 00:00:47,852
Clause one.

17
00:00:47,852 --> 00:00:48,522
The site.

18
00:00:48,522 --> 00:00:55,895
Puma Punku is part of Tiwanaku, in Bolivia, at about three thousand eight hundred and fifty metres.

19
00:00:55,895 --> 00:01:01,033
The air is thin, the winters are cold, and almost nothing grows tall.

20
00:01:05,500 --> 00:01:09,658
The name is usually translated as the Gate of the Puma.

21
00:01:09,658 --> 00:01:16,915
The platform is T-shaped, about one hundred and sixty-seven metres by one hundred and seventeen.

22
00:01:16,915 --> 00:01:20,166
A grand ceremonial stage, built for crowds.

23
00:01:20,633 --> 00:01:28,878
The first European to write about Tiwanaku was the Spanish soldier Pedro Cieza de León, in fifteen forty-nine.

24
00:01:28,878 --> 00:01:33,900
By then, the city had already been a ruin for about five centuries.

25
00:01:34,366 --> 00:01:35,927
So when was it built?

26
00:01:35,927 --> 00:01:44,401
Radiocarbon work led by Alexei Vranich puts the start of construction at about five thirty-six to six hundred A D.

27
00:01:44,401 --> 00:01:48,266
That is the early Middle Ages, not the deep ice age.

28
00:01:48,733 --> 00:01:55,869
Tiwanaku peaked between seven hundred and a thousand A D, with perhaps ten to twenty thousand people.

29
00:01:55,869 --> 00:02:01,309
Then, around the year one thousand, it collapsed, probably in a long drought.

30
00:02:01,309 --> 00:02:03,500
Puma Punku was left unfinished.

31
00:02:03,966 --> 00:02:05,735
Remember that: unfinished.

32
00:02:05,735 --> 00:02:10,768
A site that was still being built when it died is not a site left by gods.

33
00:02:10,768 --> 00:02:12,333
Clause two: the weight.

34
00:02:12,800 --> 00:02:13,573
Clause two.

35
00:02:13,573 --> 00:02:14,487
The big slab.

36
00:02:14,487 --> 00:02:23,417
The largest sandstone block on the platform is seven point eight metres long, five point two wide, and just over a metre thick.

37
00:02:23,417 --> 00:02:26,933
That is roughly one hundred and thirty-one tonnes.

38
00:02:27,400 --> 00:02:30,609
A second slab is about eighty-five tonnes.

39
00:02:30,609 --> 00:02:31,450
Heavy, yes.

40
00:02:31,450 --> 00:02:39,933
But this is red sandstone, a soft stone, and the quarry was only about ten kilometres away, near Lake Titicaca.

41
00:02:40,400 --> 00:02:46,049
Moving a huge stone ten kilometres is a logistics problem, not a magic trick.

42
00:02:46,049 --> 00:02:50,745
Ropes, sledges, wooden rollers, ramps, and thousands of workers.

43
00:02:50,745 --> 00:02:53,166
The method is slow, but it works.

44
00:02:53,633 --> 00:02:56,522
But the harder stone is the real puzzle.

45
00:02:56,522 --> 00:03:06,199
The H blocks and fine pieces are andesite, and that came from the Copacabana Peninsula, about ninety kilometres away, across the lake.

46
00:03:06,666 --> 00:03:09,268
Ninety kilometres, including water.

47
00:03:09,268 --> 00:03:13,653
Experiments with large reed boats suggest that is possible.

48
00:03:13,653 --> 00:03:14,322
Not easy.

49
00:03:14,322 --> 00:03:14,991
Possible.

50
00:03:14,991 --> 00:03:20,566
And the Tiwanaku people were lake people, with centuries of boat knowledge.

51
00:03:21,033 --> 00:03:25,730
Weight and distance: a hard job, but humans did hard jobs.

52
00:03:25,730 --> 00:03:28,079
Stamp: open, leaning to pass.

53
00:03:28,079 --> 00:03:31,480
Clause three is the one everyone came for.

54
00:03:31,480 --> 00:03:31,966
The H.

55
00:03:32,433 --> 00:03:33,400
Clause three.

56
00:03:33,400 --> 00:03:34,293
The H block.

57
00:03:34,293 --> 00:03:39,501
Interlocking andesite pieces, with sharp inner corners and flat faces.

58
00:03:39,501 --> 00:03:45,900
Many of them form tiny blind gateways, like miniature doorways that open onto nothing.

59
00:03:46,366 --> 00:03:48,917
They look identical, but they are not.

60
00:03:48,917 --> 00:03:55,094
Researchers found that the blocks are interchangeable in style, yet their dimensions differ.

61
00:03:55,094 --> 00:03:57,310
Close, but never machine-perfect.

62
00:03:57,310 --> 00:04:00,599
That detail matters, and we will come back to it.

63
00:04:01,066 --> 00:04:07,940
Archaeologists Jean-Pierre Protzen and Stella Nair studied the corners in the nineteen nineties.

64
00:04:07,940 --> 00:04:12,021
Their key point: Inca stonework has rounded inner angles.

65
00:04:12,021 --> 00:04:17,033
Here, the inside angles are crisp, and that is odd for hammered stone.

66
00:04:17,500 --> 00:04:22,950
So how do you get a sharp inside corner in a stone harder than steel chisels?

67
00:04:22,950 --> 00:04:25,002
Not with a hammerstone alone.

68
00:04:25,002 --> 00:04:28,966
That was the real argument, and it deserves a real test.

69
00:04:29,433 --> 00:04:32,079
Which brings us to the sceptic's problem.

70
00:04:32,079 --> 00:04:38,728
If the stone cannot be cut by hammering, and nobody here had steel, something else must have been used.

71
00:04:38,728 --> 00:04:40,600
Or something else must exist.

72
00:04:41,066 --> 00:04:46,480
First evidence on the table: sharp corners, flat faces, a modular system.

73
00:04:46,480 --> 00:04:48,853
It is real, and it is beautiful.

74
00:04:48,853 --> 00:04:50,633
Clause four: the theory.

75
00:04:51,100 --> 00:04:51,944
Clause four.

76
00:04:51,944 --> 00:04:53,281
The machine theory.

77
00:04:53,281 --> 00:05:02,007
The claim is that only advanced tools, perhaps a lost civilisation or visitors from the stars, could cut stone this cleanly.

78
00:05:02,007 --> 00:05:03,766
You have seen the videos.

79
00:05:04,233 --> 00:05:07,552
It spread through books, forums, and television.

80
00:05:07,552 --> 00:05:08,174
Atlantis.

81
00:05:08,174 --> 00:05:09,488
Ancient astronauts.

82
00:05:09,488 --> 00:05:11,078
A world before the ice.

83
00:05:11,078 --> 00:05:16,333
The pitch is simple: it looks too perfect, so humans could not have done it.

84
00:05:16,800 --> 00:05:19,497
But notice what the claim leaves out.

85
00:05:19,497 --> 00:05:24,964
The people who built Tiwanaku were the ancestors of the Aymara and Quechua.

86
00:05:24,964 --> 00:05:32,399
A 2019 paper in Public Archaeology pointed out that this theory treats them as if they were incapable.

87
00:05:32,866 --> 00:05:38,439
That is the hidden assumption: that people with no iron cannot do fine work.

88
00:05:38,439 --> 00:05:46,433
Yet we have fine work from people with no iron everywhere: Egyptian granite, Maya jade, Neolithic stone axes.

89
00:05:46,899 --> 00:05:49,111
And there is a second problem.

90
00:05:49,111 --> 00:05:52,429
An alien explanation is a story with no data.

91
00:05:52,429 --> 00:05:55,304
No alien tool, no metal, no spare part.

92
00:05:55,304 --> 00:05:59,433
Every claim on the list is a feeling, not a measurement.

93
00:05:59,899 --> 00:06:02,973
Stamp for the machine theory: unsupported.

94
00:06:02,973 --> 00:06:04,875
But we owe it a fair test.

95
00:06:04,875 --> 00:06:08,021
Can anyone reproduce those corners by hand?

96
00:06:08,021 --> 00:06:08,899
Clause five.

97
00:06:09,366 --> 00:06:10,256
Clause five.

98
00:06:10,256 --> 00:06:11,368
The experiment.

99
00:06:11,368 --> 00:06:15,891
Stella Nair and a team tried to answer the question directly.

100
00:06:15,891 --> 00:06:24,566
They carved a small half-cross shape, about eight inches wide, in andesite, using only tools the Tiwanaku people had.

101
00:06:25,033 --> 00:06:28,542
Flint, obsidian, hematite and other hard stones.

102
00:06:28,542 --> 00:06:31,101
They struck, abraded, and polished.

103
00:06:31,101 --> 00:06:33,075
And the result was precise.

104
00:06:33,075 --> 00:06:33,953
Sharp edges.

105
00:06:33,953 --> 00:06:34,903
Right angles.

106
00:06:34,903 --> 00:06:40,533
About forty hours of work for a first pass, around twenty-five for an expert.

107
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:44,692
Bronze tools, by the way, did not work on andesite.

108
00:06:44,692 --> 00:06:48,674
The team found that stone tools are the ones that bite.

109
00:06:48,674 --> 00:06:54,466
So the sceptic's problem has a stone-age answer: harder stone cuts softer stone.

110
00:06:54,933 --> 00:06:58,722
But we should be honest about what the test did not do.

111
00:06:58,722 --> 00:07:02,994
One of the large flat inner surfaces was not fully replicated.

112
00:07:02,994 --> 00:07:07,266
A small block in a lab is not a thousand blocks on a mountain.

113
00:07:07,733 --> 00:07:11,343
Which is why the best clue is the blocks themselves.

114
00:07:11,343 --> 00:07:13,079
Close, but not identical.

115
00:07:13,079 --> 00:07:20,300
That suggests templates and patient copying, a prefabrication system, not a machine stamping out clones.

116
00:07:20,766 --> 00:07:22,994
So far: hands can do it, slowly.

117
00:07:22,994 --> 00:07:25,500
Clause six: the metal in the joints.

118
00:07:25,966 --> 00:07:26,737
Clause six.

119
00:07:26,737 --> 00:07:27,507
The cramps.

120
00:07:27,507 --> 00:07:31,709
Some blocks have sockets for metal clamps, shaped like an I.

121
00:07:31,709 --> 00:07:37,733
These were cast in place from a copper, arsenic and nickel bronze, then hammered cold.

122
00:07:38,199 --> 00:07:42,243
The sockets come in different shapes, L, T, U, Y and Z.

123
00:07:42,243 --> 00:07:51,066
Metallurgist Heather Lechtman's work showed this bronze is a local Andean recipe, not an import and not a mystery metal.

124
00:07:51,533 --> 00:07:57,546
A cramp found in the south canal shows it was cast right there, in a mould cut into the stone.

125
00:07:57,546 --> 00:08:01,256
Pour the metal in, let it cool, and the blocks are locked.

126
00:08:01,256 --> 00:08:02,600
Clever and practical.

127
00:08:03,066 --> 00:08:04,864
And here is a nice twist.

128
00:08:04,864 --> 00:08:09,107
Similar metal cramps are used in Egypt, Greece, and Delphi.

129
00:08:09,107 --> 00:08:11,983
Different people, same engineering idea.

130
00:08:11,983 --> 00:08:17,233
Humans keep solving problems the same way, without talking to each other.

131
00:08:17,699 --> 00:08:24,061
One more clue: the roof stones were carved to look like totora reeds, a plant of the lake.

132
00:08:24,061 --> 00:08:26,040
That is a very human choice.

133
00:08:26,040 --> 00:08:29,433
A builder copying the world around him in stone.

134
00:08:29,899 --> 00:08:32,118
Metal: local, cast, understood.

135
00:08:32,118 --> 00:08:32,977
Stamp: pass.

136
00:08:32,977 --> 00:08:33,907
Clause seven.

137
00:08:33,907 --> 00:08:37,200
What did this place look like when it was new?

138
00:08:37,666 --> 00:08:38,594
Clause seven.

139
00:08:38,594 --> 00:08:39,807
The lost colours.

140
00:08:39,807 --> 00:08:42,304
Today the stones are grey and bare.

141
00:08:42,304 --> 00:08:49,440
But reports describe them once decorated with polished metal plaques, coloured ceramics and fabrics.

142
00:08:49,440 --> 00:08:51,366
A bright ceremonial palace.

143
00:08:51,833 --> 00:08:54,679
If so, the sharp corners were not for show.

144
00:08:54,679 --> 00:08:59,180
They were for fitting plaques and panels, which need straight edges.

145
00:08:59,180 --> 00:09:01,166
The precision had a job to do.

146
00:09:01,633 --> 00:09:03,678
And there is more to find.

147
00:09:03,678 --> 00:09:08,005
A drone survey in 2016 mapped about seventeen hectares.

148
00:09:08,005 --> 00:09:10,286
Only two have been excavated.

149
00:09:10,286 --> 00:09:15,399
Ground penetrating radar shows more structures buried underneath.

150
00:09:15,866 --> 00:09:17,904
So any verdict is provisional.

151
00:09:17,904 --> 00:09:23,202
Two out of seventeen hectares is like judging a book from its first two pages.

152
00:09:23,202 --> 00:09:26,666
The best discoveries here may still be underground.

153
00:09:27,133 --> 00:09:32,531
Vranich also points to older, simpler sites nearby, Pukara and Chiripa.

154
00:09:32,531 --> 00:09:40,666
Early stone traditions in the same region, which show the skills grew slowly, step by step, over centuries.

155
00:09:41,133 --> 00:09:42,990
No sudden leap from nowhere.

156
00:09:42,990 --> 00:09:45,509
That is the strongest evidence of all.

157
00:09:45,509 --> 00:09:47,366
Clause eight: the scorecard.

158
00:09:47,833 --> 00:09:48,400
Debrief.

159
00:09:48,400 --> 00:09:49,392
The scorecard.

160
00:09:49,392 --> 00:09:52,014
Seven clauses, three possible stamps.

161
00:09:52,014 --> 00:09:57,966
Let me read them one by one, the way an inspector would, with no drama and no mercy.

162
00:09:58,433 --> 00:10:00,555
The site and the date: pass.

163
00:10:00,555 --> 00:10:07,528
Built after five thirty-six A D by Tiwanaku, a documented culture, and abandoned unfinished.

164
00:10:07,528 --> 00:10:09,953
The weight and the quarry: pass.

165
00:10:09,953 --> 00:10:12,000
Heavy, but human logistics.

166
00:10:12,466 --> 00:10:15,161
The sharp corners: pass, with a note.

167
00:10:15,161 --> 00:10:20,552
A hand-carving experiment reproduced the angles, though not every surface.

168
00:10:20,552 --> 00:10:21,791
The cramps: pass.

169
00:10:21,791 --> 00:10:23,976
A local bronze, cast in place.

170
00:10:23,976 --> 00:10:25,433
The lost look: pass.

171
00:10:25,899 --> 00:10:27,749
The machine theory: fail.

172
00:10:27,749 --> 00:10:29,747
No tool, no metal, no data.

173
00:10:29,747 --> 00:10:32,263
The ancient astronaut story: fail.

174
00:10:32,263 --> 00:10:38,700
It depends on assuming that Andean people could not do fine work, and they plainly did.

175
00:10:39,166 --> 00:10:47,536
One stamp stays open: how exactly were the largest flat inner surfaces made, and how many blocks were there in total?

176
00:10:47,536 --> 00:10:48,896
Honest archaeology.

177
00:10:48,896 --> 00:10:51,399
With a lot of it still underground.

178
00:10:51,866 --> 00:10:52,443
Verdict.

179
00:10:52,443 --> 00:10:53,523
Machined stone?

180
00:10:53,523 --> 00:10:53,740
No.

181
00:10:53,740 --> 00:10:54,820
Patient masons?

182
00:10:54,820 --> 00:10:56,045
Almost certainly.

183
00:10:56,045 --> 00:11:00,657
Hard stone, hard tools, templates, time, and an ambitious state.

184
00:11:00,657 --> 00:11:03,683
The mystery is not that it was impossible.

185
00:11:03,683 --> 00:11:06,133
The mystery is how good they were.

186
00:11:06,600 --> 00:11:09,219
It is a stranger story than aliens.

187
00:11:09,219 --> 00:11:15,805
A culture with no iron, no writing, no wheel, built these gates at four thousand metres.

188
00:11:15,805 --> 00:11:17,676
That is not less amazing.

189
00:11:17,676 --> 00:11:18,500
It is more.

190
00:11:18,966 --> 00:11:24,794
Next case: a bright light, a long radio signal, and a number scribbled in red ink.

191
00:11:24,794 --> 00:11:28,632
A message from deep space, or just a good coincidence?

192
00:11:28,632 --> 00:11:30,266
That is the Wow signal.

193
00:11:30,733 --> 00:11:31,821
What do you think?

194
00:11:31,821 --> 00:11:36,899
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery put to the test.
